Root cause:
The bug was introduced when the PPS calculation logic was altered in a previous commit, which inadvertently prevented the calculation from being performed if the test time was less than one second. This change was not intentional and led to incorrect behavior in the application. Additionally, the use of a hardcoded value for the default count instead of a constant reduced code clarity and maintainability.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (time_interval / NANOSEC_PER_SEC)
    pps = cnt_send / ((double)time_interval / NANOSEC_PER_SEC);
```
Reason:
The condition `if (time_interval / NANOSEC_PER_SEC)` prevents the calculation of packets per second (PPS) when the test time is less than one second, which is an unintended change from the previous logic. This results in PPS not being calculated at all for short test durations, leading to incorrect behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
pps = cnt_send / ((double)time_interval / NANOSEC_PER_SEC);
params->count  = DEFAULT_COUNT;
```
Reason:
The first statement removes the conditional check, ensuring that PPS is always calculated regardless of the test duration. The second statement replaces the hardcoded value for `count` with `DEFAULT_COUNT`, improving code clarity and maintainability by using a defined constant instead of a magic number.


